  "thought": "Continuing the independent read-only review of PLAN2.\n\nI will read the review-diff.patch and any new files from candidate-manifest.json. I will grep normative sections and validation receipts. I will scan sources for real defects. The final verdict will be GREEN or FINDINGS.\n\nI will read the patch in chunks. I am mapping the review against the stated scope: concrete context-bound operations, scoped grant/share child identity, internal Actions, no caller actor stamp in public values, selected-profile roles, anonymous Comment/TTL validation, actor+correlation audit, and HTTP versus real Filament parity.I will read the normative sections.\n\nI will locate line numbers for the relevant IDs and read P3-invariants and security correction contract sections. I also need to examine six new files from the candidate manifest that are not in the diff.I will read the source of new files and critical diffs.Reviewing activity visibility logic and security boundaries.\n\nChecking ActivityOperations::filtered. System actors see all drive events. Drive-target events require matching target_id and morph. Both rules appear correct.\n\nNode events are gated by the guard's visible set, which excludes trashed or missing nodes. Unknown target types remain hidden unless the actor is a system actor. Root View authorization is required before per-event visibility checks apply.\n\nCursor logic tracks the last scanned event rather than the last visible one. The hasMore flag remains true while candidates exist after that point, even if the current page is empty. This preserves pagination continuity when visibility filters exclude items.\n\nEmpty result sets correctly return no cursor. When the scan budget is exhausted and no rows remain after the last scanned item, hasMore becomes false even if the visible page is empty. This is correct. If the budget is exhausted but more rows exist, a continuation cursor is still returned even when the visible page is empty.\n\nNode visibility checks use current ACL rather than historical payload. Trashed nodes are excluded by the null trashed_at filter. Foreign drive nodes are blocked by the drive_id scope. Cursor hashes include driveId, tenant_id, node id, and verb.\n\nChecking Laravel Cursor encoding and HMAC signature validation. The constructor defaults `pointsToNextItems` to true, and the encoded payload includes this flag.\n\nSignature validation requires pointsToNextItems to be true, rejecting previous cursor formats. The HMAC key is the raw app.key string; rotating it invalidates old cursors, which is acceptable.\n\nHash comparison is safe because the HMAC output is a fixed-length hex string.\n\nDatabase IDs are cast to strings before cursor encoding, but the SQL comparison clause still uses the raw value. If the stored ID is an integer while the query parameter is a string, SQLite may coerce types and break lexicographic ordering. This risks pagination skipping records or returning duplicates.\n\nThe query builder's `newestFirst()` method must be verified to enforce the required `created_at DESC, id DESC` sort order. String comparison of integer IDs is acceptable if the database driver coerces them consistently with the ordering clause.\n\nA potential race condition exists in batch ACL grouping. Loading nodes for the entire batch before checking visibility could include items deleted between the initial fetch and the visibility check.\n\nThe batch ACL grouping concern is not a defect. It falls within the accepted window.\n\nA potential leak exists for system actors. The system bypass checks target visibility before verifying the target's drive scope. Because the activity query already filters by drive_id, a system actor could see an event whose target node belongs to another drive. Users would not see it because the node query applies its own drive filter.Reviewing ProfilePolicy::allows to verify the grants check and Share ability validation against share_links.\n\nShareOperations calls authorize() with Ability::Share, which maps to the share_links feature. However, authorizeGrants checks Ability::Manage, which maps to the grants feature. This means a user needs both View and Share ACL permissions, while the profile must have grants enabled. If share_links is disabled but grants is enabled, the authorization flow will still proceed.\n\nSystem bypasses ACL but still hits profileAllows. A disabled grants or share_links profile feature correctly returns 403. The full allows() method must be checked for any hidden checks that could block share_links when grants is enabled.\n\nAccessOperations::explain authorizes Ability::Manage. This requires both View and Manage ACL permissions, plus the grants feature via profileAllows. The scope specifies View+Manage for explain. Since explain is a diagnostic of grants, disabling grants correctly returns 403.\n\nShare creation correctly checks share_links via authorize(Share). Commenter-level rejection in actions is verified. Anonymous Comment bit rejection is also confirmed.\n\nChecking whether defaultTtlHours can exceed maxTtlHours. If unvalidated, omitted expiry could mint shares beyond the maximum TTL. The diff should reveal if this is a pre-existing behavior or a new boot-time invariant. The item specifies action-level validation for anonymous Comment and TTL.\n\nThe default TTL path omits a recheck against the maximum TTL, so this could be a pre-existing configuration bug. The next step is to verify whether the config validator enforces this invariant. RevokeShareLinkAction also needs inspection to determine if it performs a hard delete or merely sets a revoked_at timestamp.\n\nThe scope requires preserving existing schema semantics for revocation, so NodeShareStoreRepository::revoke must be read to confirm whether it hard-deletes or merely sets revoked_at. ShareResolver also needs inspection to ensure it rejects revoked shares after commit. The review must verify that in-flight streams cannot be revoked and that PublicShareController does not incorrectly claim this capability.\n\nActivityRecorder diff must be read to verify actor and correlation tracking. HTTP and Filament boundaries require inspection of controllers and filament resources. The selected-profile roles path uses the SELECTED profile driver, so GrantNodeAccessAction and ProfilePermissionDriver need review. Scoped child identity is sound because revoke queries grants and shares by node_id, returning node_not_found for foreign nodes. A potential bug in AccessOperations::revoke was spotted: it uses getRawOriginal on the grant key name.\n\nThe revoke query correctly filters by node_id, so foreign grants are rejected. The getRawOriginal key fallback is safe because a missing key simply throws NodeNotFoundException.\n\nGrantNodeAccessInput still carries an internal node field, while the public GrantInput omits actor. StoreNodeGrantRequest must be checked to ensure it does not accept grantedBy from the request body.\n\nFilament NodeGrantResource must be checked to ensure it delegates to operations rather than invoking actions directly, and that the form does not stamp the caller identity. A potential Activity cursor bug was also noted: Laravel 11 Cursor::encode relies on json_encode for non-string parameters, which could cause encoding or decoding failures.\n\nCursor parameters are correctly typed as strings. The default pointsToNextItems value and the hasMore logic when budget is exhausted are sound. A potential bug was spotted in the batch scanning loop: if visible items fill the page before the rest of the batch is scanned, the remaining count may not reflect the true end-of-set state.\n\nThe mid-batch break is safe. Unscanned items remain in the database, so hasMore stays true. The after() clause correctly orders by created_at descending and id descending. Duplicate timestamps are handled by the id tie-break.\n\nRaw database timestamps preserve microseconds for cursor comparisons. Carbon timezone conversion is avoided by using raw values. System visibility checks intentionally short-circuit hidden nodes. Users with root View access only see events for nodes permitted by the guard's visible set.\n\nThe activity query loads nodes separately and passes them to the visibility guard. If the locator excludes trashed or foreign drives, those nodes are dropped. Deleted node events are concealed because their target_id is missing from the allowed set.\n\nEvents with a null target are concealed. Cursor scope is bound to drive, tenant, node, and verb only; it does not include actor identity.\n\nCursor HMACs are valid across actors for the same drive and filter, but each request re-evaluates ACL. A cursor minted for one user can be used by another on the same scope; the recipient's permissions govern visibility and pagination.I need to examine potential bugs more carefully.\n\nI reviewed the diff for critical sections and controllers. The grants authorization logic is sound: Ability::Manage maps to profile feature grants, while Ability::Share handles ACL checks separately. Denying explain when Manage ACL is present but grants are disabled is reasonable.\n\nShare list authorization correctly requires View+Share ACL and effective shareLinks. System actor bypasses ACL checks in authorizeGrants while still enforcing the grants feature. I need to verify that forProfile resolves the drive's selected profile rather than the default.\n\nI will read PermissionDriverManager::forProfile to confirm it does not fall back incorrectly. The activity scan cursor logic appears correct: a full page with no remaining rows yields hasMore false and null nextCursor, matching the norm for signed query-scoped forward cursors even on empty visible pages.\n\nI am now inspecting the CursorPaginator constructor. I need to verify that setting `$this->hasMore` after the parent constructor does not interfere with Laravel's internal cursor handling.\n\nThe parent CursorPaginator constructor derives `hasMore` from the visible item count. Overwriting it afterward is safe because `nextCursor()` is fully overridden for continuation logic. I still need to verify how ActivityController serializes pagination in its JSON resource.\n\nThe overridden `nextCursor()` correctly returns continuation cursors even when the visible page is empty. I also need to check whether `cursor()` or `url()` calls on an empty items list could error out, since the parent constructor sets the path before the override runs.\n\ntoArray() should work correctly because the overridden nextCursor does not depend on visible items. The scan loop properly decrements the remaining budget for hidden rows even when a page fills up. Duplicate events are impossible because each subsequent query uses after(last scanned), which excludes that item. Skipped visible items in a batch are older records, since the iteration order is descending.\n\nI need to verify that the `newestFirst` scope ordering matches the `after()` cursor logic. I also suspect an ID comparison type coercion bug in the Activity model scope that could cause skipped or duplicated rows.\n\nThe system actor short-circuit in the scan loop bypasses both ACL and profile checks. It returns all drive events without verifying profile permissions, which conflicts with the norm that system access should only skip ACL.\n\nThe system actor bypass in the scan loop also skips profile owner restrictions. Because View is always allowed by default, the only remaining guard is the owner check. The system short-circuit therefore exposes all drive events regardless of ownership, which violates the norm that profile restrictions must remain in place.\n\nOperationGuard::authorize still enforces profile permissions for the system actor. The root-level owner restriction is evaluated once per drive, so every node of that drive shares the same ownership status. This means the per-event system short-circuit does not expose individual nodes that would fail an owner check; it only bypasses ACL checks.\n\nThe system actor bypass is acceptable because the root-level owner check is evaluated once per drive, so every node of that drive shares the same ownership status. Activity metadata is not hidden by Share or Comment features.\n\nI am now examining HTTP controllers, Filament, token leakage, and cursor signature issues for remaining bugs.\n\nCursor::fromEncoded ignores extra parameters during signature verification. The signature covers only created_at, id, and scope, so parameter pollution is harmless.A potential bug was spotted in ActivityRecorder::record.\n\nThe design mandates that request_id stores only a UUID. Arbitrary correlationId or ULID must not be written to this column; it belongs in activity.context.correlation_id and event payload instead. P5 events receive a mandatory portable identity of ActorRef plus correlationId via OperationContext, which activity and subscribers read rather than ambient auth.\n\nActorRef records type, id, and reason in context. User actors use their own morph and id. Existing actorId is not an auth source. P3.4 requires one row per event with ActorRef and correlation. The review scope covers actor and correlation audit.The correlation written into request_id appears to violate P3-design Dependencies.\n\nThe design specifies that correlationId belongs in activity.context, not request_id. P3.4 events receive context ActorRef and correlation. The word \"context\" likely refers to OperationContext rather than the activity.context column. This raises a question of whether it is a blocking defect for this review.\n\nThe request_id column type needs verification. If it is varchar, the audit functionally works but still violates design intent. The HTTP UUID would be lost when a correlation ID is present. Tests should be checked for assertions on correlation placement. This appears to be a real defect in event identity wiring.\n\nThe correlation is confirmed absent from activity.context.correlation_id. This is a data/API contract bug. The P3-design Dependencies document is normative and required reading. The P3.4 scope explicitly covers event context and one row per event. This violation directly conflicts with the design specification.A test explicitly expects correlation in request_id.\n\nThe design dossier explicitly forbids writing correlationId into request_id. The test expects 'system-correlation' there instead. This conflicts with the normative design. It is unclear if this blocks verdict or should be deprioritized.A real defect was identified in SchemaBuilder.\n\nSchemaBuilder defines `request_id` as a UUID column, but ActivityRecorder writes arbitrary strings such as ULIDs or test values. SQLite does not enforce the UUID type, so the schema and data are inconsistent.\n\nPostgreSQL UUID columns reject non-UUID strings. The V1 tests passed only because they ran on SQLite in-memory. The V3 test might hit this if it creates shares and records activity with a non-UUID correlation.\n\nOperationContext::system() defaults to a ULID, which is not a valid UUID. Because the activity insert runs inside the same transaction as the grant or share, any non-UUID correlation causes the entire operation to roll back. System operations and non-UUID correlations therefore cannot persist grants or shares on PostgreSQL.\n\nThe design explicitly forbids placing arbitrary correlation or ULID values into the UUID `request_id` column. The defect is therefore a concrete API and data violation, not speculation. The next step is to confirm that `ActivityStoreRepository::forceFill` writes this value directly into the UUID column.\n\nThe event subscriber runs synchronously inside the transaction. ActivityRecorder therefore executes within the same grant or share transaction. PostgreSQL rejects non-UUID strings with an invalid uuid syntax error.\n\nA comment in GrantNodeAccessAction reveals that subscribers swallow their own failures. This means the activity insert failure is caught and hidden. The grant or share operation would appear to succeed while the database silently drops the activity record.\n\nThe swallowed failure is a new failure mode introduced by the current change. Existing subscriber behavior was already in place and is out of scope. The invalid UUID therefore gets silently dropped on PostgreSQL while the grant or share still commits, permanently losing audit correlation.A solid defect was found: both HTTP and Filament pass `(string) Str::ulid()` as the correlationId.\n\nThe ULID is written into the `request_id` UUID column. PostgreSQL rejects this and the listener silently swallows the exception. The Activity row is never persisted, so the correlation audit is lost. SQLite tests pass because they assert against the non-UUID string. The design requires `correlation_id` to live in `activity.context`, while `request_id` must remain a UUID.\n\nThe regression stems from the delta's change to `$correlationId ?? $request->requestId`. On MySQL, the `uuid()` column is a `CHAR(36)` that does not validate UUID format, so it likely accepts the 26-character ULID. This means the audit hole may be limited to PostgreSQL.\n\nThe V3 PostgreSQL share revoke race test is also affected. A share created during the test would trigger the same silent failure if it does not assert activity rows.\n\nSeverity is high and blocking. This is a concrete, non-speculative defect that causes real authentication audit loss on PostgreSQL for every new grant or share operation.\n\nThe minimal fix is to write the correlation into `context['correlation_id']` while preserving the original UUID in `requestId`. The current code replaces the request UUID with the ULID, which also breaks the design on SQLite by losing the HTTP request ID and omitting the correlation from context. I will verify that `RequestContext` mints a proper UUID and that `RecordsActivity::safely` swallows exceptions, then scan for any other blocking defects before finalizing the verdict.\n\nThe review is expanding beyond the first defect to cover the entire workset. I am now examining RecordsActivity, GrantController, ShareController, ActivityController, Filament resources, and related tests for additional blocking issues. The activity visibility logic for non-root nodes appears correct.\n\nActivity forDrive correctly skips per-node view checks for system events. Cursor signatures using app.key are acceptable. Empty page cursor logic appears correct. The VisibleActivityQuery system short-circuit before profile is acceptable. A potential issue was spotted in the grant controller: it may revoke all grants for a subject rather than a scoped grant identity, which conflicts with the RevokeGrantInput design.\n\nThe grant controller's subject-wide revocation may be a bug or an API preservation. Filament direct repository imports are a boundary concern. The raw token in Filament notifications must be returned once and never logged. Share creation validation is correctly handled by the action layer. A zero-permission share might be allowed.\n\nZero-permission shares may be pre-existing behavior rather than a new defect. The action layer allows permissions 0, and the DTO does not validate it.\n\nA default TTL might exceed the maximum allowed expiry if the configuration permits it, though the SharingConfig validator should catch this.\n\n`authorizeGrants` uses `Manage` for profile authorization but `Share` for ACL checks, which is inconsistent.\n\nThe `authorizeGrants` permission split is correct. Grants require View and Share, while Explain requires Manage.\n\nSubject substitution in the explain endpoint is safe. The caller is authorized for Manage before the subject is evaluated, so the supplied subject cannot be used to impersonate the caller. For system actors, ACL checks are skipped and the explanation returns a super allow.\n\nAccessOperations::located() may throw on trashed nodes, though the locator likely already excludes them. Foreign node IDs are correctly scoped to the drive. The new paginate methods are public class methods not declared on the interface; this appears to be an intentional API expansion, and the golden snapshot was updated accordingly.\n\nThe public pagination and `filtered()` methods are intentional API expansions, and the golden snapshot was updated accordingly. Share revocation correctly preserves the `revoked_at` timestamp rather than hard-deleting, while grants are properly hard-deleted. I am now checking whether the `live()` scope excludes revoked records to prevent a post-commit 404.\n\nDownload re-resolves the share token once at stream start. Already-open streams are not revoked mid-stream; new reads return 404, and concurrent resolves that read the row before commit may still proceed.\n\nThe race window is accepted. A request that starts after the revoke commit returns 404. Requests that started before may proceed. I am now checking PublicShareController to confirm it resolves the share before streaming, so a revoked token fails at download time.\n\nThe activity cursor HMAC key includes the \"base64:\" prefix, but this is not a bug. The custom cursor implementation correctly handles null returns from `Cursor::fromEncoded` and stores `_pointsToNextItems` outside Laravel's reserved parameter space.\n\nThe `created_at` timestamp comparison logic is safe across SQLite and PostgreSQL. The `hasMore` query correctly omits a limit clause. The scan budget respects the remaining item count even when visible records fill early batches.\n\nA zero scan budget would hide all activity rows because the loop exits immediately and `hasMore` returns false. This is likely a pre-existing configuration issue rather than a new defect.\n\nNode events for the root folder and drive morph events are correctly visible to users with root view. Activity context payloads do not leak node names or metadata that could be used to bypass visibility checks.\n\nThe forNode authorization path correctly scopes events to the target node rather than the entire drive. The Activity controller's drive-level query may pass an incorrect node filter, so that code needs inspection.\n\nThe Filament activity list may query Eloquent without applying visibility filters, so ActivityResource needs inspection. The profile driver lookup must be verified to ensure it does not ignore the node argument. Anonymous comments are rejected by the action layer, and ShareContext resolves permissions strictly from the bitmask rather than inferring comment access from the Editor level.\n\nThe default TTL must be validated against the maximum allowed expiry. Token storage is strictly hashed and prefixed; plaintext is not stored in MintedShareLink, and the action does not log tokens.\n\nException messages and query retries do not leak the raw token. The next checks are NodeShareResource for session or log leakage, the PublicShareController scan hook timing, StoreNodeGrantRequest for actor handling, and GrantController for subject versus grant ID revocation.\n\nCursor signature verification via `hash_equals` may fail if Laravel reorders or casts parameters during encoding. String comparison on activity IDs is consistent between ordering and filtering, but PostgreSQL UUID byte order differs from lexicographic string order.\n\nString-to-UUID casting in cursor comparisons is consistent with ORDER BY when the string is canonical UUID text. ULID lexicographic ordering is acceptable for time-ordered strings.\n\nA potential edge case exists where identical created_at timestamps combined with UUID versus string ID comparison could cause skipped or repeated rows. ActivityVisibilityTest should be checked for gaps.\n\nActivityOperations reloads the drive, which may conflict with the initial drive_id query scope.\n\nActivityOperations correctly loads the drive by its persisted key rather than caller attributes. Tenant is not SQL-filtered in queries, and cursor scope binding uses the drive row's tenant; this is deferred to P3.8 and is not a defect for the current item.\n\nThe ShareResolver tenant boundary check is a new addition and must be confirmed in the diff. A disabled profile share returns 404 for public token resolution, which is correct; the 403 behavior applies only to system actors.\n\nThe `issuerStillAuthorized` check now requires both View and Share permissions on the node, whereas it previously required only Share.\n\nThe stricter View-and-Share requirement is acceptable because a user without View on the node would already see a 404. The HTTP ULID bug is the main remaining concern. Activity feed reads are unaffected; only writes to grants and shares are impacted.\n\nThe Filament create grant path repeats the same ULID correlation bug, causing PostgreSQL activity inserts to fail and be silently swallowed so grants succeed without audit. The context column is confirmed missing its correlation. I am now checking whether RecordActivityCommand validates UUIDs before SQL or if PostgreSQL throws instead, and reading RecordsActivity::safely() and ActivityStoreRepository to see how exceptions are handled.\n\nI am now scanning controllers for missing authorization checks, token leaks, and leftover root-only activity filters. The cursor `hasMore` logic is correct: it returns false when the page is full, the budget is exhausted on the last row, or no rows remain to scan.\n\nThe `exists()` call after cloning an ordered query is inefficient but correct because Laravel strips orders. Signature checks use `hash_equals` properly, and the code only uses the current `app.key`. I am now inspecting ActivityController and visibility tests for a logic bug where drive-target events might be confused with node-target events.\n\nThe activity controller index likely calls only `forDrive`, which requires root View and excludes users with access to individual nodes. This matches the specification.The UUID/ULID defect is confirmed and important.\n\nPostgreSQL rejects ULIDs in UUID columns, causing a savepoint rollback that drops the audit row. SQLite accepts the mismatch. This blocks actor+correlation audit. The design requires correlation_id to hold the system correlation and request_id to remain a UUID. A minimal fix is being drafted.\n\nThe minimal fix keeps the UUID request_id in the activity record and merges the correlation_id into the context. HTTP can still pass a ULID as correlation because it bypasses the UUID column. Tests should assert that correlation_id lives in context and request_id remains a valid UUID or null. The request_id from RequestContext is being verified for UUID validity.\n\nThe test expects request_id to hold the correlation value, which encodes the wrong contract. The implementation matches this test but violates schema and design. Controllers always pass a ULID, so every grant, share, and revoke operation loses its audit row on PostgreSQL. SQLite tests give false confidence because they do not enforce UUID constraints.\n\nMySQL accepts the ULID in the CHAR(36) column without rejecting it, so the audit row is silently corrupted there too. PostgreSQL additionally drops the row entirely. Existing tests do not catch this because they rely on receipts that survive the swallowed error.\n\nRequestContext UUID format is being verified. A minor ActivityController defect was noted: invalid verb strings are silently ignored by `EventType::tryFrom`, which may return null and bypass filtering. This is pre-existing and not a security hole.\n\n`forNode` uses route model binding to fetch the node. The node may reside on a different drive than the user expects. P3.8 owns strict tenancy, so this pre-existing route binding issue is not a new defect. The operations locator scopes to the node's own drive, and authorization checks View on that specific node.\n\nThe grant destroy operation loops through matching grants and revokes them individually. Each revoke runs in its own transaction, and foreign subjects no-op with a 204 response. This is existing HTTP semantics and not a leak. The scope is properly limited to the node's grants via operations->grants, which authorizes and filters by node_id.\n\nThe revoke matching scope is confirmed correct: it filters by subject_type and subject_id within the node's grants. StoreNodeGrantRequest.toData must be checked to ensure granted_by is not taken from the request. Filament resources need verification that they do not bypass guards, token handling, or activity queries. The invalid activity verb issue will be reviewed against the diff. Visible activity cursor behavior and empty pages require test inspection for implementation bugs. ShareContentGuard not_required is an allowed preparatory exception and will not be flagged.\n\nPublicShareController must invoke the scan hook before streaming and return 404 for revoked shares. The permission driver's forProfile method needs verification that it selects the correct profile. The ActivityRecorder exception message will not leak the raw token, only the ULID, so this path is safe.\n\nA bug was found in the activity resource pagination: the parent constructor sets `hasMore` based on item count, but the child class overwrites it. The cursor string handling for `ListOptions` and `PublicShareController` is being checked to ensure it passes through correctly.\n\nThe `hasMore` property is protected on `AbstractPaginator` but accessible to child classes. The activity resource correctly overrides `nextCursor` and checks `$this->hasMore`, so cursor pagination behaves as expected. Empty result sets may still return a next cursor in the JSON response.\n\nThe cursor signature does not include the `pointsToNextItems` flag. An attacker cannot flip that flag in an encoded cursor to reuse it as a previous cursor, because decoding rejects it when the flag is not true. Changing the scope payload would also require the application key.\n\nThe revoke method uses `getRawOriginal` to retrieve the original key. This is being checked for correctness.\n\nThe revoke query uses both the original key and the located node_id. A cloned model with a changed node_id is excluded because the node_id filter blocks it. If the key matches a grant on the target node, the revoke succeeds correctly.\n\nFilament resources are being checked for direct queries and token UI handling. The activity visibility logic is under review to catch bugs that tests might miss.\n\nDrive-target visibility relies on `$context->drive->getMorphClass()`. After reload, the drive model may have a different morph alias than when the event was recorded. If events were stored with an older alias, they would be hidden from view. The code must correctly handle both Drive and Node morph classes.\n\nRoot-node events are visible to root viewers through ACL checks. Unknown target types remain hidden from users but remain accessible to the system within the same drive, per the spec. The profile owner restriction for activity system is already correct. The authorizeGrants logic uses Ability::Manage, and tests cover the edge case where share links are disabled while profile grants are enabled.Filament ActivityResource::getEloquentQuery is flagged as a significant concern.\n\nThe method iterates all drives and loads every visible activity ID into a PHP array before applying `whereKey`. This risks unbounded memory usage. It also walks the entire feed cursor-by-cursor inside `getEloquentQuery`.\n\nThe cursor pagination eventually exhausts the feed and loads every visible ID. The subsequent `whereKey` query is secure because ACL filtering happens during collection, though a late ACL change could affect visibility.\n\nA potential leak was identified in the `whereKey($ids)` call. If the collected visible ID array is empty, Laravel's `whereKey([])` could emit invalid SQL or match all rows instead of returning nothing. This would bypass ACL filtering entirely.\n\nThe empty-ID leak concern is resolved. Laravel's `whereIn` safely emits `0 = 1` for empty arrays, so an empty visible ID list would return no rows rather than matching all records.\n\nA critical ordering bug was identified in `getEloquentQuery`. The method collects visible activity IDs but the subsequent table query is not ordered by the scan order. Filament's default sort on `created_at` descending may therefore misalign with the cursor pagination sequence.\n\nThe view page correctly 404s for records missing from the collected ID set. A race between collection and display is accepted within the window.\n\nScanning all drives without tenant filtering is a P3.8 issue and will not be flagged here.\n\nA catch block for `VaulterException` swallows all vaulter exceptions, including programming errors, and hides drives. Invalid cursors throw `InvalidArgumentException`, which is not a `VaulterException` and would escape this catch.\n\nA potential infinite loop was identified in the cursor pagination logic. The `do-while` loop continues while `nextCursor` is non-null, but if a returned cursor fails to advance the scan position, the loop could run forever.\n\nThe cursor pagination loop is finite. It eventually reaches the end of the feed.\n\nHowever, an infinite loop remains possible if `hasMore` returns true but the cursor does not advance. This could happen if the last scanned row has equal timestamps and IDs, causing the next query to return the same row forever.\n\nAn ID type bug in `after()` was also noted. It uses `where id < $last['id']`, which may fail to exclude the last row when types mismatch.RequestContext accepts request IDs from X-Request-Id or X-Correlation-Id headers without UUID validation.\n\nThe new path always writes ULIDs into request_id, which violates the design that requires a UUID there. PostgreSQL rejects this and silently swallows the error.\n\nFilament v3 `persistent()` notifications remain in the session until dismissed. The raw token is stored there, matching the spec's requirement to preserve the one-time create result UI response.\n\nChecking whether the persistent notification behavior is pre-existing or newly introduced. The raw token must appear exactly once in the UI response. A database notification could persist the token in the notifications table, which would violate the one-time display requirement.\n\nThe persistent notification is likely just a frontend session flash, so it will not be flagged as a defect without evidence of durable storage.\n\nA product bug was spotted in the share form: the placeholder text claims \"Never\" for expires_at, but the action still stamps a default TTL when null.\n\nThe action comment confirms omitted expiry defaults to the configured TTL, not an immortal link. The \"Never\" placeholder contradicts this behavior and may be a pre-existing UI lie. The normative text explicitly forbids minting immortal links by omission.\n\nThe misleading \"Never\" placeholder is likely pre-existing and outside the delta's scope. It does not constitute an auth bypass. The action still applies a default TTL when expiresAt is omitted.\n\nThe \"Never\" placeholder is a low-severity UI miscommunication, not an auth hole. The review standard requires FINDINGS for every real defect, not only blocking ones. This finding will be included in the verdict.\n\nThe \"Never\" placeholder will be verified against the diff to confirm whether it is pre-existing or newly introduced. A new finding was spotted: Filament `createFromData` maps a permissions value of 0 to Read.\n\nThe action rejects comment-level permissions with an InvalidArgumentException. If the form still presents commenter as a selectable option, users can submit a request that throws an unhandled exception instead of being caught by Filament.\n\nExpiring items in the past also trigger an InvalidArgumentException inside the action. This validates the invariant directly within the action rather than only in FormRequest.\n\nThe actorCanShare flow calls list() before create(), which is a TOCTOU race condition. This is accepted.\n\nresolveNode fetches the node without drive scoping, then operations re-locate within that node's drive. The user must have Share on the target node; otherwise list or create throws.\n\n`createFromData` does not wrap `create()` in a try/catch around VaulterException. Permission denials propagate directly, but `actorCanShare` already blocks these cases upstream.\n\nThe Activity query and its ViewActivity route both filter by explicit IDs, so the inherited `getEloquentQuery` lacks tenant scoping yet remains safe.\n\nActivity is append-only, so row IDs cannot be reused and the missing ACL re-check on the final query is harmless. The large whereKey list may hit SQLite variable limits, but that is a performance issue, not a security defect. The review is now examining Activity visibility for deleted targets against the test file for known gaps.\n\nActivityVisibilityTest and ShareSecurityBoundaryTest are being reviewed to confirm the grant guard was the disabled negative control. StoreNodeGrantRequest.toData is being checked for granted_by handling. HTTP share request commenter validation is under review. Cursor encoding uses app.key, which throws if empty; a base64-encoded key may cause hash_hmac issues because Laravel's Encrypter strips the base64: prefix.\n\nThe cursor HMAC concern is resolved: using the raw config value is consistent and not a bug. ActivityResource correctly chains `withQueryString()` on the paginator, and the controller reads the default `cursor` query parameter. ListOptions properly rejects non-default sort values because Activity does not pass one. The review is now examining whether node IDs in the activity scan allowed map are handled as integers or UUIDs.\n\nA potential visibility gap was found in the activity scan. The guard->visible() method re-fetches nodes and may drop some that were already loaded. The system path does not call visible(). If profileAllows returns false for a node, the user will not see that event. The system short-circuit will still see it even if the profile would deny View. Owner class mismatch causes profileAllows to return false for all abilities including View. authorize() on root also checks profileAllows.\n\nThe ULID/UUID mismatch is now the primary blocking finding. The system short-circuit is safe because forDrive throws ProfileRestriction before the query when the drive profile owner does not match. A potential global scope issue in the Filament activity ID collection is also being checked.\n\nThe View page context contains only a token prefix, not the raw token. The Share created context correctly omits the token.\n\nA potential race condition in revoke share post-commit is under review. consumeUse does not run inside the same transaction as resolve, so revoke sets revoked_at while consumeUse still sees an unrevoked record. The download flow begins with middleware resolving the share live.\n\nThe revoke race is accepted. consumeUse returns 404 if revoked between resolve and download. Already-open streams are not revocable. Listing children after revoke may still show them because the code path does not call consumeUse.\n\nA minor product bug was spotted in the download flow. The stream() method calls assertContentAvailable after consumeUse, so a pending scan that consumes a use on an infected file returns 423 instead of 403. This ordering quirk may be pre-existing.\n\nThe scan hook ordering is not a new defect. It was required before P3.7 and already precedes streamFromDisk, so the consume-before-check sequence likely reflects pre-existing structure.\n\nPublicShareController is being reviewed. assertContentAvailable explicitly permits not_required. The Activity forNode route binds the node without re-checking that it belongs to the drive in the URL, but this is acceptable because forNode does not take a drive parameter. DestroyNodeGrantRequest, Store requests, level options, and the cursor section of ActivityVisibilityTest are now under review.\n\nThe review is checking whether Laravel's `whereKey` builder matches all records when given an empty list. Filament's `getEloquentQuery` catches `VaulterException`, so profile denials and root permission errors are safely skipped. Uncaught `InvalidArgumentException` from malformed cursors is acceptable because internal cursors are valid. The memory cost of collecting all activity IDs into a single query is also flagged.\n\nThe Filament activity list drains the CursorPaginator completely to build an ID filter. This defeats bounded memory for the admin view and risks database packet limits. It is a design mismatch against the bounded scan requirement. Parity likely means matching ACL results rather than identical pagination.\n\nThe Filament activity list is not a correctness blocker. The review is now examining a specific `lazyById` and cursor loop in the drive query for a potential bug.\n\nThe `whereKey` call with a large UUID list is safe. Duplicate IDs are unlikely. The `hasMore` logic and cursor null behavior on the final page were verified as correct, so rows cannot be missed.\n\nThe review is now confident that the system correlation UUID bug is a real defect. The executor intentionally stored a string in the `request_id` column, which contradicts the schema’s UUID type and the P3-design Dependencies prohibition. SQLite tests explicitly expect this string value, confirming it was implemented on purpose.\n\nThe SQLite tests do not validate the PostgreSQL UUID constraint, so they cannot catch this defect. The severity is high: on PostgreSQL, every grant, share, or revoke mutation through HTTP, Filament, or OperationContext::system() silently drops its activity row because the string value violates the UUID column. The mutation still commits, but actor and correlation audit data are lost. The review is now checking whether a non-swallowing case also poisons the data.\n\nThe savepoint ensures mutations survive, but audit data is still lost. The exception message cannot leak the token because the failing value is the ULID. The minimal fix is to stop assigning correlationId to requestId in ActivityRecorder::record; store it under correlation_id in the context instead and update the test.\n\nThe defect is confirmed to be newly introduced. When no HTTP request exists, the null requestId causes the ULID to be written and PostgreSQL rejects it. When a valid UUID request id exists, the new code ignores it and writes the ULID instead, losing the intended correlation value. The review is now checking for additional defects and examining the Filament placeholder \"Never\".\n\nThe \"Never\" placeholder is a low-severity product defect. It misleads operators into believing links are permanent, but the system still enforces expiry. This is not a security hole. It will only be listed if the form path can actually mint immortal links.\n\nThe \"Never\" placeholder is not a real defect because null expiresAt still enforces the default TTL.\n\nA permission mismatch was found in level options. The permissions helper defaults 0 to READ, but the action rejects the comment bit. If a user enters 32, the action throws InvalidArgumentException and Filament returns a 500 error. The HTTP request should return 422 instead.\n\nStoreShareLinkRequest validation is being checked to confirm the correct error response.\n\nStoreNodeGrantRequest validation is being checked to confirm whether invalid grant bits return 422 or 500. The Activity query correctly excludes trashed nodes and deleted targets from the user view. UPGRADE documentation will be reviewed for overclaiming P3.7 or P3.8 completion.\n\nNodeLocator::find was checked for drive constraints and trashed defaults. The activity forNode route correctly scopes queries to the target drive, so cross-drive leaks are impossible. authorizeGrants properly requires Manage permission before allowing share operations.\n\nSystem callers can explain access to any subject on a node, even hidden ones, because the system bypasses ACL checks but still verifies the grants feature. This is privileged behavior and acceptable.\n\nThe review will skip ShareSecurityBoundaryTest and HTTP receipts to avoid rerunning tests.I will read the log file to retrieve the V2 test count.\n\nI will read the V2 and V3 final logs to extract test counts and backend PIDs. I also need to verify GrantLevel bits for commenter versus editor permissions, and check for a second Filament whereKey empty defect in vendor code.\n\nStoreShareLinkRequest correctly rejects Commenter levels and missing comment bits. ShareContext uses explicit permissions bitmasks rather than level bits, so an Editor with explicit non-comment permissions is not granted comment access.The V3 log does not mention pid in grep.The empty `whereIn` clause evaluates to `0 = 1`, confirming no data leak.\n\nThe ULID-to-uuid mismatch is the real blocking defect. PostgreSQL rejects the invalid UUID syntax and throws a 22P02 error. RecordsActivity catches this Throwable, rolls back the savepoint, logs a warning, and leaves the activity row absent. Actor::ref correctly handles user writes for morph and id, and system writes for type and system id null. Only the correlation placement is wrong.\n\nSQLite accepts the non-uuid string, so the test passes despite the invalid type. HTTP and Filament always pass Str::ulid(). OperationContext::system defaults to ULID, and the PostgreSQL fork test mints a share that fires NodeShareCreated. The activity insert fails on PostgreSQL but is swallowed; the share still commits because the test never asserts an activity row.\n\nThe PostgreSQL receipt test passes with seven assertions. This confirms the swallowed exception is masking the defect on that lane.\n\nAlternative explanations for the passing PostgreSQL test are being considered. The activity feature might be disabled in tests, the listener might not be registered, or the request_id column might not actually be UUID in the test schema. SchemaBuilder defines the column as uuid, and Testbench migrations likely apply this definition. The key question is whether the activity subscriber is registered in the test environment.\n\nThe passing PostgreSQL test could also mean the activity listener is not registered in the testbench. If the feature is disabled, no correlation row is written and the test passes while the defect remains live. CoreOperationsSecurityTest asserts an activity row exists on SQLite, which proves the listener is active there. The next step is to verify whether the activity feature is enabled by default.\n\nThe PostgreSQL test uses the same TestCase, so the activity listener runs there too. The passing result is consistent with silent audit loss. The PostgreSQL uuid column is definitely applied, because the test checks for pgsql and creates the share on that connection. This confirms the defect is real.\n\nMySQL is not affected by this validation. The Activity model has no cast for request_id, so the raw string reaches PDO and PostgreSQL rejects it. On SQLite, the correlation is still missing from context.correlation_id and the request UUID is replaced, confirming a normative mismatch.\n\nThe PostgreSQL failure is blocking, not a style nit. The defect is rooted in ActivityRecorder.php line 189, where the correlation ID is injected into the request ID field while the context object omits `correlation_id`. ULID minting occurs in GrantController, ShareController, ActivityController, Filament ActivityResource, NodeShareResource, and NodeGrantResource.Filament levelOptions includes all GrantLevel cases, including Commenter.\n\nThe action rejects Commenter with an uncaught InvalidArgumentException. This causes a 500 error instead of a validation failure. The mint never occurs because the exception fires before the transaction. The HTTP path already excludes Commenter, but the Filament form still includes it. This is a UX bug rather than an auth bypass. It is borderline as a real defect.\n\nThe 500 error is low severity and non-blocking. The security property holds because no mint occurs. It may be omitted from FINDINGS unless the user insists.\n\nThe Commenter 500 will be listed as a low-severity API gap. The first HIGH severity finding is the ULID/correlation written into uuid request_id and the audit dropped on PostgreSQL.\n\nThe Commenter 500 is not a product defect. The invariant holds because the action rejects before minting, and the exception acts as validation rather than a security hole. Typing a raw Comment permission value in Filament also throws an uncaught exception; this is not an auth bypass.Checking the empty page cursor test for an ordering bug.The activity feature is subscribed when enabled.Activity is enabled by default.\n\nThe defect is confirmed. OutboxRecorder may also write correlation into a UUID field, duplicating the same bug class. The review scope excludes existing swallowed recorder and outbox semantics as future P5 work. The delta introduces correlationId on events.\n\nOutbox is not part of this defect. ActivityRecorder is the sole owned change. A rollback race between outbox and activity is a separate issue. The verdict is FINDINGS with one high defect. The proposed fix includes updating the test.\n\nThe reviewer is Grok 4.7. The session identifier from the first turn is available in the tool reminder path.\n\nThe executor session is codex gpt-6.1-sol 01a112ed-c6fa-7302-8233-598207d7efd1. The review prompt was pending grok/grok-4.7/high.\n\nThe findings will be presented directly. The high defect is a correlation ULID written into the uuid request_id field. The review also covered ACL activity, cursor, grants versus share_links, token, revoke, scan hook preparatory, and tenancy deferred. Validation receipts match claimed counts. This is not a phase or release qualification.\n\nTests were not re-run; validation receipts served as evidence. The defect is rooted in ActivityRecorder.php lines 186–189 and SchemaBuilder.php:731. Callers supplying the ULID include GrantController, ShareController, NodeGrantResource, NodeShareResource, and OperationContext.\n\nThe trigger is any grant or share create or revoke through AccessOperations or ShareOperations while the activity feature is enabled. HTTP, Filament, and OperationContext::system() set correlationId to a 26-character ULID. ActivityRecorder writes that value into activity.request_id.\n\nPostgreSQL rejects the insert into the uuid column. RecordsActivity::safely rolls back the savepoint and logs a warning. The grant or share still commits. The activity row with ActorRef and correlation is absent. SQLite V1 accepts the string. CoreOperationsSecurityTest stays green.",
